<.start_page current="path">
  <section
    id="start-lede"
    class="mb-10"
  >
    <h1 class="mb-3 text-3xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-gray-900 dark:text-white">
      Start building on Bitcoin
    </h1>
    <p class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300">
      Bitblocks is the read side of an application: blocks, transactions and proofs from the chain, over a plain API.
      Here is the path from a first request to something of your own.
      Each step says what works today and where it leads.
    </p>
  </section>

  <%!-- What's here: the one thing to know before relying on any of it --%>
  <section
    :if={@coverage}
    id="start-coverage"
    aria-labelledby="start-coverage-heading"
    class="mb-10 rounded-lg border border-gray-200 p-4 dark:border-gray-700"
  >
    <h2
      id="start-coverage-heading"
      class="mb-1 text-sm font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-white"
    >
      What's here today
    </h2>
    <p class="text-sm text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300">
      Every transaction is stored through block
      <span class="font-mono tabular-nums">{format_number(@coverage.through)}</span>
      of <span class="font-mono tabular-nums">{format_number(@coverage.tip)}</span> known.
      The sync works upward from the first block, so the examples below use a block that's already here.
    </p>
    <div
      aria-hidden="true"
      class="mt-3 h-1.5 overflow-hidden rounded bg-gray-200 dark:bg-gray-700"
    >
      <div
        class="h-full bg-orange-500 dark:bg-orange-400"
        style={"width: #{@coverage.percent}%"}
      ></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <%!-- The path --%>
  <section
    id="path"
    aria-labelledby="path-heading"
    class="mb-14"
  >
    <.strand_heading id="path-heading">
      The path
    </.strand_heading>
    <p class="mb-6 text-sm text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400">
      Five steps.
      The first three need no account.
    </p>
    <ol
      role="list"
      class="list-none"
    >
      <.step
        id="step-first-request"
        n={1}
        title="Make your first request"
        status="today"
      >
        <p>
          Ask Bitblocks where it stands.
          This endpoint is open, so it needs no account and no key.
        </p>
        <.code_panel
          label="In a terminal"
          code={curl_status()}
        />
        <.code_panel
          label="The response, abridged"
          code={status_example(@coverage)}
        />
        <p>
          <code class="font-mono text-xs">complete_through</code>
          is the highest block with every block below it stored and checked.
          It's worth reading first in anything you build, because it says how far the answers go.
        </p>
      </.step>

      <.step
        id="step-read-a-transaction"
        n={2}
        title="Read a transaction"
        status="today"
      >
        <p>
          The first spend ever made is in block 170: 10 coins to Hal Finney, and 40 back to Satoshi.
          <.text_link
            id="start-tx-page"
            href={"/transactions/#{@example_txid}"}
          >
            Open it on Bitblocks
          </.text_link>
          and read it for yourself.
        </p>
      </.step>

      <.step
        id="step-prove-it"
        n={3}
        title="Prove it yourself"
        status="today"
      >
        <p>
          A proof lets you check a transaction is in a block without trusting Bitblocks.
          Here is the proof for that transaction.
        </p>
        <.code_panel
          label="The API's response, abridged"
          code={proof_example()}
        />
        <p>
          Now climb from the transaction to the root.
          This needs Python and no account, and it prints <code class="font-mono text-xs">True</code>.
        </p>
        <.code_panel
          label="check_proof.py"
          code={proof_check()}
        />
        <p>
          The root is in the block's header, so anyone holding the headers can make the same check.
          <.text_link href="/start/api#proofs">The API reference</.text_link>
          covers proofs and BEEF.
        </p>
      </.step>

      <.step
        id="step-sign-in"
        n={4}
        title="Sign in and call the API"
        status="signed_in"
      >
        <p
          :if={@current_user}
          id="start-signed-in"
        >
          You're signed in as <span class="font-mono">{@current_user.login}</span>, so the API is open in this browser.
        </p>
        <p :if={!@current_user}>
          <.text_link
            id="start-sign-in"
            href={@sign_in_path}
          >
            Sign in with GitHub
          </.text_link>
          and you'll come back here.
          Bitblocks asks GitHub for your public profile and nothing more.
        </p>
        <p>
          Signed in, the whole API is a URL you can open in a tab, at
          <span class="font-mono tabular-nums">{format_number(@rate_limits.signed_in)}</span>
          requests a minute.
          It's free, and <.text_link href="/pricing">pricing</.text_link> says what comes after.
        </p>
        <p :if={@current_user}>
          Try
          <.text_link
            id="start-tx-link"
            href={tx_path()}
          >
            the transaction as JSON
          </.text_link>
          and
          <.text_link
            id="start-proof-link"
            href={proof_path()}
          >
            its proof
          </.text_link>.
        </p>
        <.code_panel
          label="GET /api/v1/txs/:txid, abridged"
          code={tx_example()}
        />
      </.step>

      <.step
        id="step-get-a-key"
        n={5}
        title="Get a key"
        status="signed_in"
        last
      >
        <p>
          A server can't sign in with GitHub, so an application needs a key.
          Each GitHub account can hold one trial key, good for
          <span class="font-mono tabular-nums">{format_number(@rate_limits.trial_key)}</span>
          requests a minute.
        </p>
        <p :if={!@current_user}>
          Sign in above, and you can get yours here.
        </p>
        <div
          :if={@new_token}
          id="start-new-key"
          class="space-y-2"
        >
          <p>
            Copy your key now.
            We keep only its hash, so this is the one time you'll see it.
          </p>
          <.code_panel
            label="Your first call with your key"
            code={key_example(@new_token)}
          />
        </div>
        <button
          :if={@current_user && !@trial_key}
          id="start-get-key"
          type="button"
          phx-click="get_key"
          phx-disable-with="Getting your key..."
          class="rounded-lg bg-blue-600 px-4 py-2 text-sm font-semibold text-white hover:bg-blue-700"
        >
          Get a key
        </button>
        <div
          :if={@trial_key && !@new_token}
          id="start-your-key"
          class="space-y-2"
        >
          <p>
            Your key starts <span class="font-mono">{@trial_key.prefix}</span>.
            If you've lost it, replace it, and the old one stops working within a minute.
          </p>
          <button
            id="start-replace-key"
            type="button"
            phx-click="replace_key"
            data-confirm="Replace your key? The old one will stop working."
            class="rounded-lg border border-gray-300 px-4 py-2 text-sm font-semibold hover:bg-gray-100 dark:border-gray-600 dark:hover:bg-gray-800"
          >
            Replace my key
          </button>
        </div>
        <p
          :if={@key_error}
          id="start-key-error"
          role="alert"
        >
          {@key_error}
        </p>
        <.code_panel
          :if={!@new_token}
          label="How a key is sent"
          code={key_example()}
        />
      </.step>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <%!-- Demos: real things, read from the index --%>
  <section
    id="demos"
    aria-labelledby="demos-heading"
    class="mb-14"
  >
    <.strand_heading id="demos-heading">
      See what's been built
    </.strand_heading>
    <p class="mb-6 text-sm text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400">
      These run on the same index as the steps above, and they're all open to browse.
    </p>

    <article
      id="demo-rexxies"
      class="mb-6 rounded-lg border border-gray-200 p-5 dark:border-gray-700"
    >
      <h3 class="mb-3 text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-white">
        <a
          href="/collections/rexxies"
          class="text-blue-700 hover:text-blue-900 dark:text-blue-400 dark:hover:text-blue-300"
        >
          Rexxies
        </a>
      </h3>
      <ul
        :if={@rexxie_items != []}
        id="rexxie-strip"
        role="list"
        class="mb-4 grid grid-cols-3 gap-2 sm:grid-cols-6"
      >
        <li
          :for={item <- @rexxie_items}
          class="aspect-square overflow-hidden rounded bg-gray-50 dark:bg-gray-800"
        >
          <.item_art item={item} />
        </li>
      </ul>
      <div class="space-y-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300">
        <p :if={@rexxies && @rexxies.items > 0}>
          <span class="font-mono tabular-nums">{format_number(@rexxies.items)}</span>
          dinosaur NFTs, each a Run jig, minted from block 771,246.
          Bitblocks proves each one's membership from the collection's mint chain, and has followed
          <span class="font-mono tabular-nums">{format_number(@rexxies.traced)}</span>
          of them to a settled answer.
        </p>
        <p :if={!@rexxies || @rexxies.items == 0}>
          Dinosaur NFTs, each a Run jig, minted from block 771,246.
          This instance hasn't indexed them yet.
        </p>
        <p :if={@rexxies && @rexxies.ended > 0}>
          For <span class="font-mono tabular-nums">{format_number(@rexxies.ended)}</span>
          of them the trail ends, and
          <.text_link href="https://github.com/afomi/bitblocks/blob/develop/docs/field-notes/2026-11-02-rexxies-spent-outside-run.md">our field note</.text_link>
          looks at how that happens.
        </p>
        <p>
          Where this data comes from: Rexxies sit well above where the full sync has reached, so a collection is imported on its own, and each transaction is checked against its txid.
          The pictures come from the collection's catalog, because the on-chain payloads are encrypted.
        </p>
        <p>
          Run's SDK is
          <.text_link href="https://github.com/runonbitcoin/run-sdk">kept for reference</.text_link>
          and no longer supported, so read Rexxies as a demo of what the chain keeps, and start new work from the list below.
        </p>
      </div>
    </article>

    <ul
      role="list"
      class="grid grid-cols-1 gap-3 sm:grid-cols-2"
    >
      <li :for={
        {id, href, title, blurb} <- [
          {"demo-tardinians", "/collections/grand-tardinians", "Grand Tardinians",
           if(@tardinians && @tardinians.items > 0,
             do: "#{format_number(@tardinians.items)} items on 1Sat, with membership and holders from the chain.",
             else: "A 1Sat collection, with membership and holders from the chain."
           )},
          {"demo-bitcom", "/bitcom", "Bitcom registry",
           "Namespaces on chain, with proof of who owns each one."},
          {"demo-types", "/types", "Transaction types",
           "What each kind of transaction is, how it's spent, and real examples."},
          {"demo-metanet", "/metanet", "Metanet",
           "Walk Metanet trees from a root to its children and versions."}
        ]
      }>
        <a
          id={id}
          href={href}
          class="block h-full rounded-lg border border-gray-200 p-4 hover:border-orange-400 dark:border-gray-700"
        >
          <span class="block font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-white">
            {title}
          </span>
          <span class="mt-1 block text-sm text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400">
            {blurb}
          </span>
        </a>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p class="mt-4 text-sm text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400">
      These are beta views, and they fill in as the sync climbs.
      For more of what people are building,
      <.text_link href="https://bsvradar.com">BSV Radar</.text_link>
      is a public directory of Bitcoin applications.
    </p>
  </section>

  <%!-- References: other people's work, plain links --%>
  <section
    id="references"
    aria-labelledby="references-heading"
    class="mb-14"
  >
    <.strand_heading id="references-heading">
      Start from these
    </.strand_heading>
    <p class="mb-2 text-sm text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400">
      Good starting points from around the ecosystem.
      None of them is ours, and we checked each link on 2026-11-05.
    </p>
    <h3 class="mt-6 text-sm font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-white">
      Smart contracts
    </h3>
    <ul
      role="list"
      class="divide-y divide-gray-200 dark:divide-gray-700"
    >
      <.out_link
        name="sCrypt boilerplate"
        href="https://github.com/sCrypt-Inc/boilerplate"
      >
        A collection of example contracts with tests, in TypeScript.
        Clone it, run the tests, change a contract.
        Its last commit was in 2024, so pair it with the documentation below.
      </.out_link>
      <.out_link
        name="sCrypt documentation"
        href="https://docs.scrypt.io/bsv-docs/overview/"
      >
        How to install sCrypt and write contracts in TypeScript, with worked tutorials.
      </.out_link>
    </ul>
    <h3 class="mt-6 text-sm font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-white">
      Applications and wallets
    </h3>
    <ul
      role="list"
      class="divide-y divide-gray-200 dark:divide-gray-700"
    >
      <.out_link
        name="BSV Skills Center quick start"
        href="https://docs.bsvblockchain.org/intro/quick-start"
      >
        Set up a Node project with the SDK.
      </.out_link>
      <.out_link
        name="BSV TypeScript stack"
        href="https://github.com/bsv-blockchain/ts-stack"
      >
        The source of <code class="font-mono text-xs">@bsv/sdk</code>, with wallet tooling, overlays and messaging.
      </.out_link>
      <.out_link
        name="1Sat SDK"
        href="https://github.com/b-open-io/1sat-sdk"
      >
        Tools for building on 1Sat Ordinals: NFTs, fungible tokens and on-chain data.
      </.out_link>
      <.out_link
        name="Yours Wallet"
        href="https://github.com/yours-org/yours-wallet"
      >
        A non-custodial, open-source wallet for Bitcoin and 1Sat Ordinals.
      </.out_link>
    </ul>
    <h3 class="mt-6 text-sm font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-white">
      Standards and data
    </h3>
    <ul
      role="list"
      class="divide-y divide-gray-200 dark:divide-gray-700"
    >
      <.out_link
        name="BRCs"
        href="https://github.com/bsv-blockchain/BRCs"
      >
        Bitcoin Requests for Comments: published standards for wallets, tokens, transactions and more.
      </.out_link>
      <.out_link
        name="1Sat Ordinals specification"
        href="https://docs.1satordinals.com/"
      >
        The protocol behind Grand Tardinians and the other 1Sat collections.
      </.out_link>
      <.out_link
        name="WhatsOnChain API"
        href="https://docs.whatsonchain.com/"
      >
        A REST API for blocks, transactions and addresses, and a good companion for anything above where our sync has reached.
      </.out_link>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <%!-- Writing: the other half of an application --%>
  <section
    id="write"
    aria-labelledby="write-heading"
    class="mb-14"
  >
    <.strand_heading id="write-heading">
      Write with Qart
    </.strand_heading>
    <div class="space-y-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300">
      <p>
        Bitblocks reads, and doesn't compose or sign.
        Qart, a sibling project, is a write side you can use today: tools for selling from a web page you own.
        A payment goes straight to the seller's address, and Qart takes no percentage of a sale.
      </p>
      <ol
        id="qart-steps"
        class="list-decimal space-y-1 pl-5"
      >
        <li>
          Sign in at <.text_link href="https://qart.app/start">qart.app/start</.text_link> and set up a shop.
        </li>
        <li>
          Add a button to any page you own, with the key from your shop.
        </li>
        <li>
          Sell something, then read the payment's transaction on Bitblocks once the sync reaches it.
        </li>
      </ol>
      <.code_panel
        label="A Qart buy button, in plain HTML"
        code={qart_button()}
      />
      <p>
        <.text_link href="https://teambitcoin.us">Team Bitcoin</.text_link>
        is a store built this way, with these buttons on its page.
      </p>
    </div>
    <dl class="mt-5 divide-y divide-gray-200 border-y border-gray-200 text-sm dark:divide-gray-700 dark:border-gray-700">
      <div class="py-3 sm:flex sm:gap-4">
        <dt class="w-28 shrink-0 text-xs font-semibold text-green-700 dark:text-green-400">
          Live
        </dt>
        <dd
          id="qart-live"
          class="text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300"
        >
          Buy buttons, pay links, receipts, a point of sale, double-entry books, HTTP 402 pay-per-request, and a wallet API that broadcasts transactions your own tools signed.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div class="py-3 sm:flex sm:gap-4">
        <dt class="w-28 shrink-0 text-xs font-semibold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200">
          On testnet
        </dt>
        <dd
          id="qart-testnet"
          class="text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300"
        >
          Escrow, pre-signed refunds and co-signed transactions.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div class="py-3 sm:flex sm:gap-4">
        <dt class="w-28 shrink-0 text-xs font-semibold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200">
          In preview
        </dt>
        <dd
          id="qart-preview"
          class="text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300"
        >
          Minting your own BSV-21 token, and referral covenants.
        </dd>
      </div>
    </dl>
    <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400">
      These labels are Qart's own, from <.text_link href="https://qart.app/start">its ladder</.text_link>.
      <.text_link href="https://qart.app/build">qart.app/build</.text_link>
      explains the thinking, and <.text_link href="https://qart.app/llms.txt">llms.txt</.text_link> lists its API.
    </p>
  </section>

  <%!-- Guidance: a way into the views page --%>
  <section
    id="guidance"
    aria-labelledby="guidance-heading"
    class="mb-8"
  >
    <.strand_heading id="guidance-heading">
      What we'd tell a friend
    </.strand_heading>
    <p class="mb-3 text-sm text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400">
      Our draft views, with our reasons, dated so you can see how they age.
    </p>
    <dl class="divide-y divide-gray-200 border-y border-gray-200 dark:divide-gray-700 dark:border-gray-700">
      <div
        :for={{anchor, title, line} <- guidance_topics()}
        class="flex flex-col gap-1 py-3 sm:flex-row sm:items-baseline sm:gap-4"
      >
        <dt class="w-44 shrink-0 text-sm">
          <a
            href={"/start/guidance##{anchor}"}
            class="text-blue-700 hover:text-blue-900 dark:text-blue-400 dark:hover:text-blue-300"
          >
            {title}
          </a>
        </dt>
        <dd class="text-sm text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300">
          {line}
        </dd>
      </div>
    </dl>
  </section>
</.start_page>
